Classification Performance Summary (Part 2 – Iris Dataset)
The classification task focused on predicting the species of iris flowers (Setosa, Versicolor, Virginica) using four machine learning models. Overall, all models demonstrated high accuracy, with minor differences in class-wise performance.

📋 Model Comparison Summary

Model	Accuracy	Kappa	Setosa Sensitivity	Versicolor Sensitivity	Virginica Sensitivity	Balanced Accuracy (avg)
KNN	97.78%	0.9667	1.0000	1.0000	0.9333	0.9778
Linear Regression	82.22%	0.7333	1.0000	0.6667	0.8000	0.8667
Decision Tree	93.33%	0.9000	1.0000	0.9333	0.8667	0.9333
Logistic Regression	95.56%	0.9333	1.0000	1.0000	0.8667	0.9556
✅ Key Observations:
Setosa was consistently classified with 100% accuracy across all models.
KNN had the best overall performance, with near-perfect results and a balanced accuracy of 97.78%.
Logistic Regression also performed excellently, especially in distinguishing Versicolor and Virginica.
Linear Regression was the weakest of the four, mainly due to lower sensitivity for Versicolor and Virginica.
All models achieved high specificity, especially for Setosa and Virginica.
📌 Insights:
KNN is highly effective on small, well-separated datasets like Iris, likely benefiting from the distance-based separation of clusters.
Decision Trees and Logistic Regression are both reliable choices with solid interpretability and generalization.
Linear Regression, while useful in regression problems, is less suited for multi-class classification due to its continuous output nature.
